  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday morning through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 158 PM EDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nSaturday starts with the pattern in transition from a weak westerly \nflow to a full fledged North to northwest flow around a significant \nwestern ridge setting up.  500mb flow within the models while fairly \nconsistent at indicating a disturbance within the flow moving \nour direction, are very inconsistent in how convection evolves\nalong and ahead of that disturbance. Of the CAMS which reach into\nearly Saturday many suggest a TS complex diving SE through the \nmid MS valley but differ on how and where the bulk of the activity\nis located. As a result, NBM pop guidance for Sat is fairly high \n~60pct but the spread is fairly significant amongst the many \nmembers ranging from 10pct to 70pct...likely due to the \nuncertainty the NW flow pattern brings. This yields a fairly low\nconfidence pop forecast for Saturday ranging everywhere from\nmostly dry to decent storm coverage.\n\nLikewise, the remainder of the extended period from Sunday to \nWednesday locks the area into a solid N to NW flow aloft...and a \nmore uncertain pattern in terms of sensible weather. Ensemble \nmeans of the GFS and ECMWF attempt to identify individual\nvorticity maxima sliding SE around the eastern periphery of the \nwestern ridge suggesting that some \"waves\" will be present, but \nsuspect the blending within the means are dampening any singular \nsignal out. Single model runs, (GFS,CMC,ECM etc) all suggest \ndisturbances, aka ridge riders, within the mean NW flow rotate \naround the ridge and southeast into the area during the period. \nGiven the pattern and the instability in place, would not be \nsurprised to see shower/storm chances increase each day with time.\nCurrent WPC and NBM guidance is less than 10 pops Monday and \nTuesday, but the spread within the guidance ranges from 0 to 30pct\nmeaning some of the guidance is catching onto the prospect of \nactivity in the flow. From prior history of summer ridges, ala \nsummer of 2023, we need to keep our guard up. CSU and NCAR ML \nguidance also recognizes the pattern and at least suggest some \nsevere probs possible..esp Tue and Wed with deep N flow and plenty\nof instability. In short, will need to keep an eye on Mon- Wed \nperiod for severe risk within the NW flow regime. \n\nDespite being in NW flow, the surface dpts remain elevated in the \nlow to mid 70s through the period.  Combined with increasing temps, \nexpect Apparent T's to slowly increase and peak by Tue and Wed. \nBaring relief from rain, guidance suggests Heat Advisory is likely \nneed Mon - Wed across a large portion of the fcst area, but \nparticularly along and south of I-20 where HI values range from 105-\n108 each day.",
